Abstract

The author constructs the "Generative Theory of the Seven Mysteries" (also known as the "Xuan Generative System"), a novel cognitive framework for natural language processing grounded in the Eastern Daoist yin-yang cosmology, focusing on innovative pathways for linguistic research from an Eastern philosophical perspective. Building upon the author’s interdisciplinary expertise and practical experience, the study centers on two fundamental forms—"motion carrier" and "Dynamic-Static Self-Regulating Entity"—and proposes three core solutions: first, employing the four aspectual markers (habitual, progressive, perfect, and perfect progressive) as uniaxial motion trajectory markers within a 3+1 dimensional yin-yang dual-axis system to achieve linguistic mapping of spatiotemporal states; second, redefining the core functions of "will" and "would" as conveying intention, volition, and motion direction, thereby decoupling their conventional association with specific temporal dimensions; third, establishing an integrated spatiotemporal–linguistic Xuan model featuring a hyper-five-dimensional nested 4+1 space (loop-dimensional space), the "Xuan-Jing-Sheng Diagram" (Well Chart, a visual coordinate-based cognitive framework of the theoretical core), and the spatiotemporal spiral angle (Xuan Arc Angle). The hyper-five-dimensional model extends classical four-dimensional spacetime by incorporating a dynamic interface comprising virtual, real, and Xuan layers, with the spatiotemporal spiral angle serving as a metric for delineating the Xuan layer boundary between past and present. The integrated spatiotemporal–linguistic Xuan model enables deep fusion of temporal logic and language generation. Presented as a conjecture within the Pengchen Wei Cosmic Conjecture, this logically self-consistent and empirically verifiable theory has demonstrated breakthrough explanatory power over traditional grammatical theories through more than three decades of practical application, infusing natural language processing with innovative momentum derived from Pengchen Wei’s Eastern cosmological perspective.

