1. Introduction

Drawing on decades of interdisciplinary expertise and hands-on experience, this study presents the first systematic exposition of the original theoretical framework "Xuan Generation" —specifically the "Generative Theory of the Seven Mysteries" (also known as *Pengchen Wei’s Heart Sutra of Language Generation*). Its core operational logic corresponds to the "Xuan Algorithm," a logical language processing algorithm built upon the theoretical framework of Xuan Generation (Generative Theory of the Seven Mysteries), emphasizing the transformation of theoretical models into linguistic applications. This paper addresses only the theoretical aspects, without delving into specific implementation pathways or technical parameters. As the founder of the Eastern Philosophical Cognitive-Generative Linguistics theory and the founder and chief theorist of Beijing Youdi Technology Co., Ltd., the author transcends the limitations of conventional linguistic theories by grounding the framework in cosmic natural laws, integrating Eastern philosophy, astronomical cognition, and practical experience. The resulting original Xuan Generation language system possesses both scholarly depth and practical value, aiming to explore new pathways for linguistic research and multilingual applications.

Traditional linguistic theories often prioritize academic interpretation, exhibiting common shortcomings such as "emphasis on theory over practice" and "preference for Western logic over Eastern wisdom". The Xuan Generation framework centers on the "cosmology–natural law–language generation" tripartite cognitive structure, breaking down disciplinary barriers and incorporating the Daoist philosophical core of "the Dao generates all things" and dialectical thinking into language research. It not only fills a theoretical gap in the application of Eastern cosmology to language generation but also translates abstract philosophical insights into explorable, applicable linguistic cognitive methods through practical implementation, thereby establishing a distinct theoretical advantage.

2. Core Theoretical Framework and Cognitive System 

2.1 Theoretical Core and Foundational Cognition 

The "Generative Theory of the Seven Mysteries" posits that the essence of language generation lies in the synergistic mapping of symbols and semantic logic, reflecting the natural operational principles of the universe in linguistic expression. The core theoretical foundation of the "Pengchen Wei Cosmic Conjecture—Spacetime Helix Angle (Mystery Arc Angle)" derives from the author's decades of interdisciplinary research and practical exploration. In constructing this framework, the author achieves a profound integration of Eastern philosophy, astronomy, and linguistic application, forming a distinctive cognitive system. This interdisciplinary synthesis is not a mere superposition but is grounded in the fundamental logic of cosmic natural laws. It deeply intertwines the astronomical understanding of the helical motion of celestial bodies (yin-yang dynamics) with Daoist yin-yang philosophy and the logic of language generation, ensuring the theory is both academically grounded and pragmatically relevant. 

2.2 Motion Carriers and Dynamic-Static Self-Regulating Entity 

The study centers on two foundational forms: "motion carriers" and "Dynamic-Static Self-Regulating Entity", which serve as the core supports of the linguistic framework under the "Generative Theory of the Seven Mysteries". A "motion carrier" refers to the spacetime medium that underpins the existence and movement of objects, while a "Dynamic-Static Self-Regulating Entity" denotes an agent capable of autonomously modulating states of rest, motion, and stasis-dynamics interplay. These two forms establish a relational frame of reference, providing the underlying logic for describing spacetime states in language and acting as a critical bridge connecting cosmic perspectives with language generation. 

2.3 Grammatical Interpretation of *Will* and *Would* 

This theoretical framework offers a clear resolution to the grammatical functions of *will* and *would*. Aligned with the core cognition that time dimensions are limited to the present and past, the primary role of *will* and *would* is not to delineate time but to convey ideas, intentions, plans, and directional forward movement, devoid of independent temporal attributes. Specifically, *would* focuses on expressing subjective intentions and directional movement in past contexts, while *will* corresponds to analogous expressions in present scenarios. Both serve to communicate the states of "Dynamic-Static Self-Regulating Entity", thereby fundamentally challenging the traditional grammatical misconception that ties them to specific temporal dimensions. 

The "4+1 Nested Hyper-Five-Dimensional (Loop-Dimensional) Space" model, the Spacetime Helix Angle, and the Language-Xuan Spatiotemporal Model collectively constitute a spacetime cognition solution for the linguistic framework. These components mutually reinforce one another, forming an integrated system that uniformly supports the theoretical unification of cosmic cognition and language generation. 

2.4 Monaxial Motion Trajectory Marking 

The marking of monaxial motion trajectories along the yin-yang dual axes within the 3+1-dimensional spacetime is a core solution for state description in the linguistic framework. The four categories of markers—habitual, progressive, perfect, and perfect progressive—essentially represent precise linguistic mappings of the stasis, motion, and stasis-dynamics composite states of "self-regulating entities of stasis-dynamics" relative to "motion carriers." Each marker corresponds to specific trajectory characteristics of monaxial motion on the yin-yang dual axes, enabling systematic and concrete expression of objects' spacetime motion states. This approach fills a theoretical gap in the linguistic description of motion states under the guidance of an Eastern cosmic perspective.

2.5 Hyper-Five-Dimensional Nested 4+1 Space, Spatiotemporal Helix Angle, and the Language-Xuan Spatiotemporal Model 

Drawing from Pengchen Wei’s Eastern Yin-Yang cosmology, the author proposes an original “Hyper-Five-Dimensional Nested 4+1 Space (Loop-Dimensional Space) Model,” which is fundamentally a “hyper-four-dimensional +1” framework. The 4-dimensional aspect corresponds to the classical four-dimensional spacetime composed of three-dimensional space and time, where the three-dimensional space is defined with respect to the “Dynamic-Static Self-Regulating Entity” as the core reference, referring specifically to the spatial domain inhabited by the subject. Time, similarly grounded in the existence of the “Dynamic-Static Self-Regulating Entity,”essentially represents an objective record of its motion trajectory. With the “Dynamic-Static Self-Regulating Entity”serving as the spatiotemporal reference, the coordinated variation of its three-dimensional spatial position and motion trajectory (time) constitutes a unique four-dimensional space for each subject under study. The +1-dimensional aspect constitutes the original core of the model, defined as a dynamic “Xuan Layer” formed by the helical motion of the sun and moon (Yin and Yang). This layer, together with the Yin Realm, Yang Realm, Virtual Layer, and Actual Layer, forms a three-dimensional structure (corresponding to the grid-logic framework of the “Xuan-Jing-Sheng Diagram”). 

In the three-tiered grid of the “Xuan-Jing-Sheng Diagram”: above the Xuan Layer lies the Virtual Layer, which corresponds to actions and states that have not yet occurred, accommodating linguistic expressions of both “past-present” and “future-present” scenarios; below the Xuan Layer is the Actual Layer, corresponding to actions and states that have occurred (past) or are occurring (present). Here, the past exists as an independent four-dimensional space, anchoring objective and determinate trajectories outside the boundaries of the Xuan Layer within the Actual Layer, while the present anchors immediate trajectories within the boundaries of the Xuan Layer. The Xuan Layer itself, serving as the core interface for transformation between the virtual and the actual, functions both as the boundary between two 3+1-dimensional Yin-Yang spacetimes and, in essence, as the operational manifestation of “Dao” within spacetime. Its primary function is to bear subjective cognitions and transformative intents such as conjecture, inference, judgment, and requirement. 

Regarding the Yin-Yang dual realms in the grid of the “Xuan-Jing-Sheng Diagram”: to the left of the Yin axis of the Xuan Layer lies the Yin Realm, corresponding to the past (what has occurred, encompassing the grid areas associated with *would*, *could*, *did*); to the right of the Yin axis lies the Yang Realm, corresponding to the present (what is occurring, encompassing the grid areas associated with *will*, *can*, *do*). When the motion trajectory of the “Dynamic-Static Self-Regulating Entity” crosses the Xuan Layer via the spatiotemporal helix angle (Xuan Arc Angle) and enters the Yin Realm, it completes the spatiotemporal transformation from “present→ past,” entering another four-dimensional space independent of the current spacetime (corresponding to the past spacetime beyond the left side of the Yin axis). This structure aligns fully with the temporal classification of “past, past-present, present, future-present” and the core concept of the “eternal now,”providing a robust spatiotemporal framework supporting the view that the temporal dimension consists solely of past and present, with the past constituting an independent four-dimensional space. 

The “Xuan-Jing-Sheng Diagram” – Spatiotemporal Helix Angle (Xuan Arc Angle) serves as a core cognitive parameter, essentially functioning as a boundary metric for the Xuan Layer space, naturally formed by the helical motion of the Xuan Layer. Its primary function is to distinguish temporal attributes (past/present). It acts both as a key node for the Xuan Layer’s regulation of virtual-actual transformation and as the fundamental basis for temporally locating action trajectories in linguistic expression. By detecting angular variations in the helical motion of the Xuan Layer, one can precisely determine whether an action belongs to the past (Yin Realm) or present (Yang Realm), thereby offering a perceptible and interpretable cognitive dimension for defining temporal attributes. 

Details of theoretical application: The Language-Xuan Spatiotemporal Model corresponds to the core +1-dimensional model described above. It serves as a theoretical vehicle for deeply integrating the logic of hyper-five-dimensional nested 4+1 space and the boundary function of the spatiotemporal helix angle with language generation. Its core logic accurately resonates with the concept of “temporal notions in spatial terms”—namely, the “eternal now”—and is fully congruent with the temporal classification of “past, past-present, present, future-present.” The “eternal now” is not a single temporal point but an integrated spatiotemporal category encompassing these four temporal forms. Combined with the three-dimensional structure of the Xuan Layer (virtual-actual layer), Virtual Layer, and Actual Layer in the “Xuan-Jing-Sheng Diagram” within the hyper-five-dimensional nested 4+1 space model, it forms a closed-loop semantic system: In the “Xuan-Jing-Sheng Diagram,” the past, as an independent four-dimensional space, belongs to the Actual Layer and lies to the left of the Yin axis of the Xuan Layer, corresponding to determinate trajectories of events that have occurred; the present belongs to the Actual Layer and spans the Xuan Layer covering the area to the right of the Yin axis, corresponding to actions or states that are occurring; the past-present and future-present are collectively attributed to the Virtual Layer, corresponding to scenarios and intents that have not yet occurred. As specific expressions within this grammatical system, they precisely meet the linguistic mapping requirements for actions in the Virtual Layer. It is essential to clarify that the temporal dimension fundamentally still consists only of past and present; “past-present” and “future-present” represent refined classifications of unmanifest states within the Virtual Layer and are not independent temporal dimensions. The functions of *will* and *would* are not to delineate time but primarily to convey ideas, intentions, plans, and the directional attribute of forward motion. Their semantic transmission relies on the Xuan Layer’s function of virtual-actual transformation, the temporal positioning of the spatiotemporal helix angle (Xuan Arc Angle), and the trajectory-marking function of motion along a single axis, ultimately achieving a deep integration of cosmic cognition, temporal classification, and language generation.

3. Theoretical Innovation and Practical Value 

3.1 Groundbreaking Innovations at the Theoretical Level 

The core innovation of this theory lies in transcending the cognitive boundaries of traditional linguistic research by establishing, for the first time, an intrinsic connection between cosmic natural laws—such as spiral spacetime motion, hyper-five-dimensional nested spaces, and the "Xuan-Jing-Sheng Diagram" visual coordinate tool—and language generation. This framework constructs an original Eastern language generation system fundamentally distinct from Western theoretical paradigms. Unlike Western linguistic theories, which emphasize grammatical rules and logical analysis, this theory is guided by the Taoist philosophical core of "the Tao generating all things," treating language generation as a microscopic projection of cosmic natural laws and achieving theoretical unification of "cosmic perspective and linguistic perspective." 

Simultaneously, the theory innovatively proposes core concepts such as "Xuan Layer" and "spacetime spiral angle," filling theoretical gaps in the Eastern philosophical understanding of spatiotemporal cognition in language. It provides an integrated cognitive pathway of "cosmos-nature-language" for linguistic studies, enriching the diversity and cultural connotation of language generation theories. 

3.2 Practical Exploration at the Applied Level 

Addressing the shortcomings of traditional linguistic theories that "prioritize academic interpretation over practical implementation," the "Xuan Generation" theory is supported by the author’s over three decades of hands-on practice in real-world contexts. It systematically translates abstract cosmic cognition and philosophical logic into applicable, reproducible, and transferable language application methods. 

Validated through decades of practical application, the theory effectively mitigates the tendency toward rote memorization in traditional language learning, helping users deepen their understanding of linguistic semantics and temporal attributes. It demonstrates strong practical adaptability, particularly in rectifying misconceptions in traditional grammar regarding the temporal attributes of "will/would." The theory conveys the core insight that "these modal verbs express ideas, intentions, plans, and directional motion rather than functioning as temporal markers," emphasizing that "the temporal dimension inherently comprises only the present and the past." This ensures targeted and practical theoretical application. 

